{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>Besides the first famine that was in the days of Abraham</b> — perhaps there had been no famine in the world until the days of Abraham (Pirkei deRabbi Eliezer 26), and therefore Scripture counts from it, for what need is there to mention this? But the correct interpretation in my eyes is that this is to tell that they would remember that first famine, and tell about it, that on account of it Abraham went down to Egypt, and there HaShem did great honor for him. And therefore Isaac desired to walk in the way of his fathers to go down there, until it was said to him, \"Do not go down to Egypt.\" And the reason for the prevention, our Rabbis said (Bereshit Rabbah 64:3): you are a perfect burnt offering, and the land outside is not worthy of you. And in my opinion there is further included in the matter a hint for the future, for Abraham's exile to Egypt because of the famine is a hint that his children would be exiled there, and his going to Abimelech was not an exile, for there he dwelt of his own will; but Isaac's going down there because of the famine hints at exile, for he was exiled from his place against his will and went to another land. Now his exile from his place to the land of the Philistines, which is the land of his father's sojourning, hints at the exile of Babylon, which is the place of their fathers' sojourning, for they were in Ur of the Chaldees. And know that the exile mentioned hints at the deed of Isaac, when they did not take his wife, and he had there only exile and fear. At first he said, \"Whoever touches this man and his wife shall surely be put to death,\" and after days he retracted and said, \"Go away from us,\" and afterward they returned to him in covenant. So too the exile of Babylon: they were exiled there because of the ravages of famine, and from the time they were there they did not enslave them nor oppress them, but their great men were princes in the kingdom, and afterward they said, \"Whoever there is among you of all his people — may his Elohim be with him, and let him go up\" (Ezra 1:3). And the officials beyond the River and the satraps warned against them, and afterward they nullified the building, and it remained nullified for a period of time; afterward they again gave permission for the building, and warned and said, \"Let offerings be made to the Elohim of heaven and let them pray for the life of the king and his sons\" (Ezra 6:10)." "<b>To Abimelech king of the Philistines</b> — it is not known whether this is the Abimelech who was in the days of Abraham, or whether every king of the Philistines at that time was called thus, for even in the days of David he is called Abimelech. And Onkelos' opinion is that he was his son. And the sense of \"And Isaac went to Abimelech\" is that he intended to go down to Egypt, and he went to Abimelech, his father's covenantal ally, perhaps he would deal well with him in the days of the drought, and he would not need to go down to Egypt. Now Abimelech, because of his covenant with Abraham, did not touch him or his household at all, but the men of the place asked him, in the manner of inquiry, about his wife, and he said, \"She is my sister.\" And even so, the king did not touch her, nor did any of his men, for they were reminded of the matter of Abraham. And therefore he said, \"One of the people might nearly have lain\" — I say: I did not touch her and kept away from her, but it was nearly the case that one of the people of the land would stumble with her, and you would have brought guilt upon us, just as there was upon us sin in the matter of your father."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {0 "The Aramaic phrase והות בטלא is rendered hyper-literally as 'and it remained nullified'; the language mimics the Aramaic of the biblical book of Ezra in which the administrative correspondence about the Temple is composed."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:35:21.952105775Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}